NNAAMMEE
     mmoouunntt__ffffss — mount a Berkeley Fast File System

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     mmoouunntt__ffffss [--oo _o_p_t_i_o_n_s] _s_p_e_c_i_a_l _n_o_d_e

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The mmoouunntt__ffffss command attaches the Berkeley Fast File System on the
     _s_p_e_c_i_a_l device on to the file system tree at point _n_o_d_e.

     This command is invoked by mount(8) when using any of the following syn‐
     tax:

         mmoouunntt [options] _s_p_e_c_i_a_l _n_o_d_e
         mmoouunntt [options] -t ffs _s_p_e_c_i_a_l _n_o_d_e

     The _s_p_e_c_i_a_l device is either a disklabel(8) UID (DUID) or an entry in
     _/_d_e_v.  If it is a DUID, it will be automatically mapped to the appropri‐
     ate entry in _/_d_e_v.  In either case the partition must be present in the
     disklabel loaded from the device.  The partition name is the last letter
     in the entry name.  For example, /dev/sd0a and 3eb7f9da875cb9ee.a both
     refer to the ‘a’ partition.

     This command is normally executed per file system by rc(8) at boot time
     using the mount(8) command.  The options are as follows:

     --oo _o_p_t_i_o_n_s
             Options are specified with a --oo flag followed by a comma sepa‐
             rated string of options.  See the mount(8) man page for possible
             options and their meanings.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     mount(2), disklabel(5), fstab(5), disklabel(8), mount(8), umount(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     A mmoouunntt__ffffss command appeared in NetBSD 1.1.

BBUUGGSS
     It is possible for a corrupted file system to cause a crash.

